Order XXIII Rule 1 CPC 1908 distinguishes between withdrawal of a suit (which may be with or without leave) and abandonment of a suit (which occurs without leave). Understanding the distinction is essential for determining the consequences and the client's rights.

Withdrawal of Suit (with leave under Rule 1(3)):
(a) Plaintiff applies for leave to withdraw;
(b) Court grants leave - subject to conditions (costs, terms);
(c) Plaintiff withdraws with liberty to file fresh suit;
(d) Fresh suit is allowed on the same subject matter;
(e) Time spent in withdrawn suit is excluded from limitation under Section 14 Limitation Act 1908 (A 1956 SC 44; A 1932 Pat. 81);
(f) Plaintiff pays costs to defendant under Rule 1(1) proviso;
(g) Withdrawal with leave preserves the right to re-litigate.

Abandonment of Suit (without leave under Rule 1(1)):
(a) Plaintiff abandons the suit without applying for leave;
(b) No formal application made - no court order;
(c) The suit is dismissed under Rule 1(1);
(d) Plaintiff is liable to pay costs to defendant under Rule 1(1);
(e) No fresh suit lies on the same subject matter under Rule 1(4);
(f) The dismissal operates as res judicata to the extent of Rule 1(4) bar;
(g) No exclusion of time under Section 14 Limitation Act - the clock runs from the date of institution.

Key differences:
(a) Application requirement: Withdrawal requires an application for leave; abandonment occurs without any formal step;
(b) Right to fresh suit: With leave - yes; without leave - no;
(c) Cost liability: Withdrawal with leave - plaintiff pays costs; abandonment - plaintiff pays costs AND loses right to fresh suit;
(d) Res judicata effect: Withdrawal with leave - no res judicata; abandonment - Rule 1(4) bar creates res judicata-like effect.

For Pakistani practitioners, the correct procedure is always to apply for leave to withdraw - even if the client wants to file a fresh suit. Failure to apply for leave before withdrawal results in the loss of the fresh suit right and the Rule 1(4) bar.

Order XXXII of the CPC 1908 extends its protective provisions not only to minors but also to persons of unsound mind - those who are legally incapacitated due to mental disorder from managing their affairs. The court's role in determining unsoundness of mind and appointing appropriate representatives is central to the Order's operation.

Persons of unsound mind under Order XXXII:
Order XXXII Rule 1 defines 'minor' as a person who has not attained majority. By analogy and by the general principle of parens patriae jurisdiction, the same protective framework applies to persons of unsound mind who are unable to manage their legal affairs - they are treated as equivalent to minors for the purposes of the Order's protective provisions per the general principle of analogous application of minor law to persons of unsound mind.

Appointment of next friend/guardian ad litem for persons of unsound mind:
(a) Where a person of unsound mind is a plaintiff - a next friend must be appointed per Rule 1;
(b) Where a person of unsound mind is a defendant - a court-appointed guardian ad litem must be appointed per Rule 1;
(c) The appointment process is identical to that for minors - the court examines the evidence, holds a hearing, and appoints the appropriate person per Rule 2.

Determining unsoundness of mind - the court's role:
(a) The court has inherent power under Section 151 CPC to examine whether a party is of unsound mind per the general inherent power jurisprudence;
(b) The court may examine the party directly, hear medical evidence, and form its own view per the general practice;
(c) The court may also refer the party to a medical board for psychiatric evaluation per the general practice;
(d) The court's determination of unsoundness of mind is a factual finding - it requires evidence of mental incapacity per the general evidentiary standard.

Consequences of proceeding without protective representation:
(a) A suit filed by a person of unsound mind without a next friend is a nullity per Rule 1;
(b) A suit defended by a person of unsound mind without a guardian ad litem is similarly defective per Rule 1;
(c) Any decree passed against a person of unsound mind without protective representation is a nullity per the general principle that a person of unsound mind cannot validly consent to a decree per the general law.

Pakistani courts' approach:
The Supreme Court of Pakistan has consistently held that persons of unsound mind must be protected in civil proceedings per the general parens patriae doctrine. The Lahore High Court and Sindh High Court have held that courts must be vigilant in identifying persons of unsound mind and appointing appropriate representatives per the general high court jurisprudence.

Order XXII CPC 1908 and Order XXIII CPC 1908 address two different categories of persons whose interests are affected by court proceedings - those who are parties to the suit (Order XXII) and those who are not parties but have an interest in the subject matter (Order XXIII).

Order XXII - Death, Marriage, Insolvency:
(a) Applies to parties to the suit - plaintiffs, defendants, legal representatives;
(b) Covers situations where a party dies, marries, or becomes insolvent during the pendency of the suit;
(c) Provides for substitution of legal representatives in place of the deceased party;
(d) Ensures that the suit continues - the right to sue survives death;
(e) Does NOT cover persons who are not parties to the suit - it deals with the procedural continuity of the suit itself;
(f) The Lahore High Court Amendment (2016) extends Order XXII to suits for possession - giving legal representatives the right to continue possession suits after the original plaintiff's death.

Order XXIII - Withdrawal and Adjustment:
(a) Applies to persons who are NOT parties to the suit - typically third parties with an interest in the subject matter (e.g., tenants, mortgagees, encumbrancers);
(b) Covers situations where a person with an interest in the subject matter wishes to withdraw from the suit or adjust their position;
(c) Provides for the court to hear such persons and pass orders affecting their interests;
(d) Does NOT provide for substitution of legal representatives - it deals with the substantive rights of non-parties;
(e) The Sindh High Court Amendment (2017) extends Order XXIII to suits for possession - allowing tenants to participate in possession suits and protect their interests.

Key Differences:
(a) Scope: Order XXII covers parties to the suit; Order XXIII covers non-parties with an interest in the subject matter;
(b) Nature of intervention: Order XXII substitutes legal representatives in the suit; Order XXIII allows non-parties to participate in the suit and protect their interests;
(c) Purpose: Order XXII ensures the suit continues; Order XXIII ensures the suit takes account of the interests of non-parties;
(d) Outcome: Order XXII results in substitution and continuation of the suit; Order XXIII results in orders affecting the non-party's interest in the subject matter.

Practical Example: In a possession suit (Order XXI), a tenant (non-party) who is displaced by the decree holder's execution sale can file an application under Order XXIII to protect their tenancy rights - the court hears the tenant and passes an order protecting their tenancy. In contrast, if the tenant's landlord (plaintiff) dies, the tenant's interest in the tenancy is protected through Order XXII substitution - the legal representatives of the deceased landlord continue the suit and the tenant's tenancy rights are addressed through the execution proceedings.
